{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvMVt4WNkSBD5Ktixtqs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PELICAN\n\nPelicans fish mainly by plunge-diving or by scooping. Brown pelicans, the diving kind, circle high over the water, spot a fish, then fold their wings and drop headfirst, often from 10 metres or more. They hit the surface at speed, and an air sac under the skin cushions the impact. Their long bill pouch fills with water and small fish as they go under. They then tip the water out by lifting the bill, and swallow the fish.\n\nAmerican white pelicans take a different approach. They fish cooperatively, forming a line or horseshoe and beating their wings on the surface to herd fish into shallow water, where each bird scoops them up.\n\nPelicans feed mostly on fish such as anchovies, sardines and menhaden, and they usually fish in groups. Their pouches can hold about three gallons of water, though they swallow only the fish, not the water."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17457,"cache_read_input_tokens":11248,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17457},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"96a70301-12f8-4b28-ad25-91091d699f34","uuid":"123d74f2-6242-4285-8630-70e6709a88a3","timestamp":"2026-10-11T11:15:45.375Z","request_id":"req_011CfvMVskuDRuFA2vn7KcmN"}
